{"data":{"updated_timestamp":"12/11/2026 17:41:33","profile":{"player_id":"87680","last_updated":"12/11/2026 17:41","fullname":"Momina Riasat","short_display_name":"M Riasat","name":"Momina Riasat","gender":"F","dob":"17/12/2002","date_of_death":null,"place_birth":"Pakistan","nationality_id":"6","nationality":"Pakistan","nationality_short_code":"PAK","nick_name":null,"player_skill":{"id":"2","text":"Bowler","role":"Bowler"},"batting_style":{"id":"2","text":"Left Handed"},"bowling_style":{"id":"15","text":"Slow left-arm orthodox"},"writeup":"<p>Calm, composed, and possessed of a deceptive left-arm spin trajectory, Momina Riasat represents the modern breed of disciplined Pakistani spinners who rely on relentless accuracy rather than flamboyant mystery. Hailing from Haripur in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, she developed<br />\r\nher game away from the traditional metropolitan cricket hubs of Lahore and Karachi. From an early age, her ability to drift the ball into right-handed batters before gripping and turning it away caught the attention of regional coaches. She brought an unfussy, workhorse attitude to every match she played, displaying a maturity and temperament well beyond her years.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>Her domestic journey began in the regional circuits where she represented Abbottabad Women and Peshawar Women in regional competitions. In the grueling environment of Pakistan domestic cricket, where opportunities can be scarce, Momina stood out by bowling tight spells that choked opposition scoring rates. Her performances in the National Women Under-19 tournaments and subsequent senior women domestic championships paved the way for higher honors. Coaches noticed her repeatable bowling action, her steady release, and her ability to land the ball on a good length ball after ball without yielding cheap boundary balls.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>As the Pakistan Cricket Board expanded its domestic tournaments, Momina transitioned into franchised and departmental setups, turning out for sides like the Western Warriors and PCB Blasters. She played a pivotal role in the Pakistan Cup Women One Day Tournament and National T20 championships, showing that she could maintain her control against seasoned international batters. Her tidy economy rates earned her a place in the Pakistan A Women squad. In emerging team tournaments and shadow tours, she consistently broke stubborn partnerships during middle overs, proving herself as an attacking weapon in low-scoring tussles.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>The years 2024 and 2025 marked a crucial period in her technical development. During the 2025 domestic season, Momina delivered several match-defining performances for the Invincibles in the National Women One Day Cup, regularly claiming multi-wicket hauls including<br />\r\nfigures such as three wickets for 20 runs against the Strikers. Her discipline during pressure situations in both 50-over and T20 games caught the eye of national selectors looking for fresh spin options. Her control of flight and pace variation improved noticeably, ensuring that batters could neither step out easily nor sit comfortably on the back foot.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>Her consistent domestic toil culminated in a deserved international call-up in early 2026. Momina made her senior international ODI debut on May 4, 2026 against Zimbabwe at the National Stadium in Karachi. She adapted swiftly to the highest level, taking four wickets across the three-match ODI series with best bowling figures of two wickets for 39 runs while keeping her economy rate well under four and a half runs per over. A few months later, on July 31, 2026, she earned her T20 debut against Sri Lanka at the Rangiri Dambulla International Stadium, where she demonstrated her knack for making inroads by claiming two wickets for 18 runs in the final fixture of the tour.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>By the second half of 2026, Momina had firmly established herself within the national squad mix as Pakistan prepared for major international competitions including the Women Asia Cup and future global tournaments. At just 23 years of age, her steady left-arm orthodox craft offers balance and variety to the national bowling attack. She provides the team management with a reliable option who can tie down one end and strike in the crucial middle overs. With her grounded personality, relentless work ethic, and growing confidence on the international stage,<br />\r\nMomina promises to be a vital pillar of the Pakistan bowling attack for many years to come.</p>","relation":null,"social_details":null,"trophy_cabinet":null,"majorteams":{"team":[{"id":"1130","active":"1","is_primary":"true","is_international":"1","text":"Pakistan"},{"id":"2053","active":"0","is_primary":"false","is_international":"0","text":"Blasters"},{"id":"2054","active":"1","is_primary":"false","is_international":"0","text":"Challengers"},{"id":"2721","active":"1","is_primary":"false","is_international":"0","text":"Pakistan A"},{"id":"3410","active":"0","is_primary":"false","is_international":"0","text":"Stars"},{"id":"4257","active":"1","is_primary":"false","is_international":"0","text":"Peshawar"}]},"international_debut":{"comp_type_id":"12","comp_type":"ODI International - f","match_id":"270514","match_center":"https://www.icc-cricket.com/matches/270514/pakistan-vs-zimbabwe","series_id":"15517","series_name":"Zimbabwe Women in Pakistan, 3 ODI Series, 2026","match_date":"04/05/2026","for":{"id":"1130","text":"Pakistan","short_name":"PAK"},"vs":{"id":"1485","text":"Zimbabwe","short_name":"ZIM"},"venue":{"id":"105","text":"National Stadium, Karachi"},"batting":{"runs":"","balls_faced":"","hundreds":"0","innings":[]},"bowling":{"wickets":"1","runs_given":"38","ball_bowled":"48","economy":"4.75","five_wk_hauls":"0","innings":[{"number":"1","wickets":"1","runs_given":"38","ball_bowled":"48","economy":"4.75","five_wk_hauls":"0"}]},"fielding":{"catches":"0","stumpings":"0","run_outs":"0","innings":[]}},"international_last_match":{"comp_type_id":"1